@startuml proto-servo
' ===========================================================================
' proto-servo.puml - (a) the hobby-servo PWM protocol engine, both sides.
'
' A servo has no clock, no framing, no acknowledgement and no checksum. The
' whole protocol is: hold the line high for between 1.0 and 2.0 ms, once every
' 20 ms. The only question a servo asks of the wire is HOW LONG it was high,
' and the only way to be wrong is to be wrong about time.
'
' firmware/servo_sweep.pe emits a five-position sweep; tb/tb_pe_soc_servo.v
' measures it on the pads and reconstructs the frame period from the WIRE, not
' from the program's intent.
' ===========================================================================

title Servo PWM - the protocol engine on both sides\n<font size=12>50 Hz frame, 1.0-2.0 ms pulse; a servo is a pulse width, nothing else</font>

hide empty description
hide stereotype
skinparam shadowing false
skinparam state {
  BackgroundColor White
  BorderColor #33555
  FontName monospaced
}
skinparam note {
  BackgroundColor #FFFBE6
  BorderColor #C8B800
  FontName monospaced
  FontSize 11
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------- EMITTER --
state "FIRMWARE SIDE - firmware/servo_sweep.pe" as EMIT {

  [*] --> TABLES
  TABLES : TABLES\ndmem[0..4] pulse n1: 97, 145, 169, 121, 193\ndmem[5..9] gap   n1: 229, 223, 31, 31, 31\ndmem[10]  the position index, 0..4 then 5

  state "IDLE\nthe line LOW" as IDLE
  IDLE : the servo's idle level is LOW, and it is\nset BEFORE the pin is claimed.\nAN IDLE LEVEL THAT IS ONE INSTRUCTION LATE\nIS PART OF THE PROTOCOL

  state "PULSE\nhold high" as PUL
  PUL : Y = 0\nTXPIN = SRV_DATA (0x40)\nthen the delay on dmem[X + 0] with\n(n2,n3) = (2,152): one outer step is\n(4*152+11) = 619 clocks = 10.3 us

  state "GAP\nhold low" as GAP
  GAP : Y = 1\nTXPIN = 0x00\nthen the delay on dmem[X + 5] with\n(n2,n3) = (11,123): one outer step is\n4655 clocks = 77.6 us

  state "NEXT\nadvance the index" as NXT
  NXT : X++\nif X == 5 the sweep is done: park\nelse back to PULSE\ndmem[10] IS THE PROGRAM'S OUTPUT:\n0 = never started, 2 = hung,\n5 = finished, and the TB reads it

  state "DLY1/DLY2/DLY3\nthe three-nested-counter delay" as DLY
  DLY : total(n1,n2,n3) = (n1-1)*(10 + (n2-1)*(4*n3+7)) + 4 clocks\nBOTH INNER COUNTERS ARE RELOADED from their\ntargets (dmem[13], dmem[12]) at the top of\nevery pass. A version without the reloads\nreads zero, subtracts one, wraps to 255, and\nproduces hundreds of thousands of clocks where\nthousands were asked for - while looking\ncompletely correct. 19 ms as NOPs would be\n1.14 M words against 1 024 words of imem.

  TABLES --> IDLE
  IDLE --> PUL : a position is due
  PUL --> DLY
  DLY --> GAP : Y = 0, a pulse finished
  GAP --> DLY
  DLY --> NXT : Y = 1, a gap finished
  NXT --> PUL : X < 5
  NXT --> [*] : X == 5
}

' ---------------------------------------------------------------- RECEIVER --
state "SERVO SIDE - tb/tb_pe_soc_servo.v" as DEC {

  [*] --> PAIR
  PAIR : each RISE is paired with the NEXT fall\nafter it, NOT with the j-th fall in the list.\nThe pin has a LEADING fall - the firmware\ndriving the line from the released pull-up high\ndown to the idle low as it claims the pin - so\ncounting falls independently pairs pulse 0 with\nthat one and shifts EVERY measurement by a pulse.

  state "WIDTHS\nfive measurements" as WID
  WID : width[j] = fall(j) - rise(j)\ncompared against want[j], in the ORDER\nthe firmware emitted them\n\npulse 0: 1000 us  0 degrees\npulse 1: 1500 us  centre\npulse 2: 1750 us\npulse 3: 1250 us\npulse 4: 2000 us  full travel\nnot monotonic on purpose

  state "FRAME\nrise to rise" as FRM
  FRM : measured ACROSS TWO DIFFERENT pulse\nwidths - 1000 us then 1500 us - because that\nis the measurement that tells a SLOT from a\nDELAY.

  state "CHECK\nwhat is claimed" as CHK
  CHK : 1. exactly 5 rising edges\n2. every width inside 1000..2001 us\n3. every width within 30 us of the REQUEST\n4. frame period inside 19900..20100 us\n5. the short gaps still exceed 1000 us\n6. dmem[10] == 5 (the firmware finished)\n7. the last edge is LOW - the line idles low\n8. the widths are pairwise DISTINCT and span\n   more than 900 us - non-vacuity

  PAIR --> WID
  WID --> FRM
  FRM --> CHK
  CHK --> PAIR : the frame repeats\n(the firmware loops)
}

EMIT -[#3A7,hidden]-> DEC : the same three instructions,\nOUT, delay, OUT

note bottom of EMIT
  <b>Why the frame is a SLOT and not a delay.</b>
  The obvious implementation is "pulse 1.5 ms, then
  wait 18.5 ms". That works until the position
  changes: a forgotten subtraction turns 50 Hz into
  45 Hz at one end of the travel and 58 Hz at the
  other. A servo notices - the position jitters
  and the motor hums.

  So each position carries its OWN gap, chosen as
  20 ms minus its own pulse, and the rise-to-rise
  interval is 20 ms whatever the pulse is. The gap
  is not "the rest of the frame", it is the frame
  minus the pulse.
end note

note bottom of DEC
  <b>Why the order is a checkable property.</b>
  A sweep that only grows can be satisfied by a
  firmware that emits the right widths in the wrong
  order, and a testbench that sorts its
  measurements before checking them cannot tell
  the difference. Emitting 1.0, 1.5, 1.75, 1.25,
  2.0 makes measurement i comparable against
  position i.
end note

note bottom of FRM
  <b>Why two positions are a full 20 ms and three are not.</b>
  Positions 0 and 1 sit a full 20 ms apart so the
  frame rate can be measured. Positions 2..4 use a
  2.5 ms gap - a fifth of that - which saves 45 ms
  of simulated time. The trade is stated rather
  than hidden: the 50 Hz claim rests on the
  MEASURED frame period, and the sweep claim rests
  on five measured pulse WIDTHS, which is all it
  needs. Nothing about the width measurement is
  weakened by the short gaps, and the TB labels
  the short ones "a short sweep gap, not a frame"
  rather than pretending they are slots.
end note

@enduml
